43:| Collections and higher-order list functions — `filter`, `reduce`, `any`, `all`, `zip`, `chunk`, `group_by`, `sort_by`, `take`, `skip`, `reverse`, `unique` | `SURVEY.md` rank 1 | `map` already calls a Redblue closure and `push` already exists; the missing functions are loops and comparisons, not native kernels. |
44:| Record field enumeration — `keys`, `values`, iterating a record | `SURVEY.md` rank 3 | A record is a language value with insertion order; exposing its fields is value semantics, not a numeric kernel. |
45:| Ordering, min/max, sum, mean, median | `SURVEY.md` rank 4 | Sorting and folding are O(n log n) and O(n) over values the interpreter already owns; the readability win is the whole point and a native sort would add a boundary for no practical gain. |
46:| Text slicing, searching and padding — `slice`, `index_of`, `pad_left`, `repeat`, `chars` | `SURVEY.md` rank 5 | `length` already counts characters in the interpreter; slicing by character is the same Unicode work, written once in Redblue. |
47:| Number formatting and parsing — fixed precision, separators, a stated base | `SURVEY.md` rank 9 | This is presentation over a number Redblue already has; formatting is arithmetic and text building, not a native dependency. |
48:| CSV and JSON writers | `SURVEY.md` rank 10 | The readers already produce Redblue lists and records; writing them back out is serialising existing `Value`s, not a new engine. |
64:| Regular expressions | `SURVEY.md` rank 2 | `regex` (already a dependency) | A correct, linear-time regex engine is a specialist native engine; hand-rolling one in Redblue would be slower, buggier and a denial-of-service risk. |
65:| Date and time arithmetic | `SURVEY.md` rank 6 | `chrono` (already a dependency) | Calendar arithmetic, leap years and time zones are a correctness problem; `chrono` already backs `time.format` and `time.unix`. |
66:| Directories, globbing and stat | `SURVEY.md` rank 7 | `walkdir`, `glob` (crates.io) | Walking the filesystem and matching paths are OS-facing operations with platform edge cases; no amount of Redblue loop removes that. |
67:| Encodings and digests | `SURVEY.md` rank 8 | `base64`, `hex`, `percent-encoding`, `sha2` (crates.io) | Bit-level conversion and hashing are native work, and a digest implemented in interpreted code is both slow and easy to get wrong. |
72:| Statistics | class — not in the survey | `statrs` (crates.io) | Distributions and special functions are numeric precision work; a Redblue implementation would be slower and less accurate. |
88:| A regular-expression engine written from scratch | Correctness, linear-time behaviour and security are specialist work. `regex` already exists and is already linked; writing our own would be slower and less safe for no readability gain. |
